            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="BAB01" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>Charles</forename>
                        <surname>Babbage</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1791-12-26">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1871-10-18"/>
                    <occupation>mathematician</occupation>
                    <occupation>computer pioneer</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>The eminent mathematician Babbage appears in the diary between 1834-5 in what seem to be fashionable soirees, hosted by
  <persName key="STA01">Leicester Stanhope</persName>, <persName key="SHE10">Lady Mary Shepherd</persName> 
     and <persName key="STE02">Lady Stepney</persName>, and there is an 'adv' meeting at the Royal
  Institution as well as a couple of meetings at Babbage's. The identification is confirmed by Hyman's <title>Charles Babbage: Pioneer of the Computer</title>'
     where there are matches for two of the hostesses mentioned above in the fashionable circles in which Babbage moved.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="other">
                        <title>DNB</title>
                    </bibl>
                    <bibl type="printedbook">Anthony Hyman, <title>Charles Babbage: Pioneer of the Computer</title> (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1982)</bibl>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
